One should offer a pair of garments, a sacred thread with an upper garment, / two shoes, O royal sage, and a parasol over the head;

food and a water pot, seven kinds of grain along with sesame seeds, / silver, cotton, milk-rice, and a signet-ring for Hari.

Or a cow should be given here, adorned with her calf, / having horns of gold, hooves of silver, and a copper back;

one should then give a bell-metal milking vessel and a tail adorned with jewels to the guru; / with devotion one should give a bed furnished with accessories to a virtuous person.

One should offer incense, a lamp, an offering of food, fruit, and leaves; / Keśava is to be worshipped by great devotees with these mantras,

along with tulasī leaves and seasonal flowers, / by the name of that month: the feet and knees to the one whose form is Viṣṇu;

at the private parts to the Lord of the Secret, at the hips to the Yellow-robed One, / at the navel to the Bearer of the Form of Brahmā, at the belly to the Womb of the Universe;

at the heart to the One Attainable through Knowledge, at the throat to the Form of Vaikuṇṭha, / at the forehead to the Upward-Moving One, on the arm to the Destroyer of Dakṣa;
